WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:06.820
There are images of police using violence
against peaceful protesters.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:14.050
There are images of police creating confrontations,
even attacking but defenseless elderly people.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:19.760
We played a video of police knocking over
an elderly man with a cane shooting at the

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:20.760
media.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.860
These images have gone viral over the weekend.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.300
Now, I want to be really clear.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.910
I already did a story about how what's going
on in America right now is not just about

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:30.910
one thing.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:31.960
It's not just about race.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.430
It's not only about police.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:37.050
It's not exclusively about capitalism or Trump.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:40.720
This is a massive, multifaceted ordeal.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.360
But that doesn't mean that we shouldn't look
at individual elements.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:49.630
And one of these elements is the idea of police
as oppressors versus police as victims, as

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:53.040
some have started framing police to be.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.320
We are seeing wholesale pepper spraying of
peaceful protesters.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.580
We've seen members of the media shot with
rubber bullets.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.930
We saw kids hit with pepper spray.

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:06.520
We've seen police ramming police vehicles
into crowds.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.400
A Dallas woman was hit in the face with a
rubber bullet while walking home with her

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:12.400
groceries.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.010
Police wildly hitting people with batons,
in some cases, people walking away from protests.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:22.909
None of this excuses any violent actions by
protesters.

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:29.430
And there have been horrible actions, including
attacking business owners, beating one business

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:34.260
owner to within an inch of his life or trying
to defend his business, beating a female business

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.290
owner brutally out in front of her store.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:41.229
We have seen it all, but I want to focus in
a little bit here on the police component

00:01:41.229 --> 00:01:42.890
and talk about it with you.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.460
There are people and I'm friends with some
of them on Facebook.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:47.240
I'm seeing it on Twitter.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.870
There are people who have immediately taken
to painting police as victims.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:57.050
Arguments like a bad cop may have killed George
Floyd in Minnesota, but now innocent police

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:00.630
all over the place are being attacked for
no reason.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.950
And often these arguments end up in debates
about numbers like, well, most police are

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.330
good ways at 97 percent or 99 percent.

00:02:08.330 --> 00:02:11.020
Well, is it ninety nine or ninety nine point
three percent?

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:15.250
And I said earlier in the show, imagine we
figured out with some precision what portion

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:20.260
of police are the problem is, we might call
it, and what portion of police are not.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:23.170
Imagine ninety nine point two percent of police
are good.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:29.459
OK, well, I imagine that most of my audience,
even if you're okay with property crime in

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:35.450
protest where insurance companies are the
ones taking ahead in corporations, I imagine

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:41.170
most of my audience is not OK with unprovoked
attacks against random police officers who

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:42.470
aren't doing anything wrong.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:48.080
If you do believe that for what happened to
George Floyd in Minnesota, it's OK to just

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:51.080
physically attack an officer in Miami.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.190
We're just not on the same page.

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:54.840
I think that's the minority of my audience.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:03:00.890
Does our analysis depend, though, on whether
it's ninety nine point two or ninety nine

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:06.629
point three percent of police that are good
guys in it for the right reasons?

00:03:06.629 --> 00:03:12.140
Because what really this is about is context
and power dynamics in total.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:17.950
Once you concede that most cops are good and
doing their job as best they can, often in

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.880
difficult situations, sometimes dangerous
situations.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:26.739
You also have some cops that go into law enforcement
specifically because they would love an opportunity

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:31.580
to beat the crap out of someone, just like
some portion of people go into the military

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:36.300
because they like the idea of shooting someone
and killing them in some kind of a theater

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.960
of war at some point.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:39.340
That is some percentage.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.340
OK, so.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:41.340
So then what?

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.340
Right.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:45.970
Whether it's point eight or point seven or
point oh eight percent of police that are

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:50.870
going in because they want to do violence
and misbehave and have all the worst intentions.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:51.870
What next?

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:55.560
And that's where the more difficult conversations
start.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:01.080
Questions like are the good cops who are just
doing their job?

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.780
Part of the problem in that if you're not
part of the solution, are you implicitly supporting

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.770
the status quo of that small percentage of
police that do horrible things and get away

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:12.770
with it?

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:20.699
To what degree should every, quote, good cop
quit in protest to show solidarity with the

00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:26.430
protests, leaving our country to be policed
only by the bad cops?

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:30.139
What counts as a sign of being complicit is
silence.

00:04:30.139 --> 00:04:32.809
It's a sign of being complicit.

00:04:32.809 --> 00:04:38.490
What power and responsibility do individual
police officers have to fix the problem versus

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:43.729
police chiefs versus federal law enforcement
agencies versus versus police unions that

00:04:43.729 --> 00:04:46.169
represent officers around the country?

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:52.379
Should the good cops be treated respectfully
or suspiciously absent any reason to believe

00:04:52.379 --> 00:04:55.099
they are one of the bad cops?

00:04:55.099 --> 00:04:56.689
How should they be treated by protesters?

00:04:56.689 --> 00:04:58.280
The average person, etc.?

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:01.669
When you start asking these questions, you
realize that most of the changes that need

00:05:01.669 --> 00:05:03.710
to be made are systemic changes.

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:08.340
It needs to be much more likely that if you
do the wrong thing as a police officer, you

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:09.999
will be caught and punished.

00:05:09.999 --> 00:05:16.360
It's not likely right now in a lot of our
society, just from this weekend, we have dozens,

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:20.619
if not hundreds of officers on video doing
horrible things.

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:25.460
Are we confident they're going to be identified
and investigated and charged and punished

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.159
when it's appropriate?

00:05:27.159 --> 00:05:32.749
Not really, because if it were more likely
that they were held accountable, they would

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:34.659
probably change their behavior.

00:05:34.659 --> 00:05:35.969
That is a systemic issue.

00:05:35.969 --> 00:05:43.860
A good system will take care of that sliver
of police that are doing sick, horrible things.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.729
So let's answer the questions one by one.

00:05:46.729 --> 00:05:50.940
First of all, being a police officer is not
like being an accountant.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:51.940
OK?

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:55.779
Accountants don't represent communities in
the way that police officers do.

00:05:55.779 --> 00:05:58.619
Accountants don't wield their power with firearms.

00:05:58.619 --> 00:06:03.659
And accountants don't have a power dynamic
relative to their clients, the way police

00:06:03.659 --> 00:06:07.199
officers have a power dynamic relative to
the people that they are dealing with.

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:13.020
So on the one hand, the good police officers
must say much more loudly.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:17.169
These things are not OK and we're committed
to fixing it and changing it and good for

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:18.169
them.

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:20.319
Many police officers have done exactly that.

00:06:20.319 --> 00:06:28.860
At the same time, the anger by protesters
towards a system of policing that is fundamentally

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:34.020
broken in many parts of our country is justified
anger.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:40.520
And this is why it's so powerful when police
officers get it and say, I'm with you, like

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:45.469
the sheriff in Michigan who marched with protesters
in the video going viral.

00:06:45.469 --> 00:06:51.889
And it's important for protesters to know
that their anger with the system is justified.

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:59.520
And yet it's not OK to go attack random individual
cops who have not done anything wrong, which

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:06.219
is why it was great to see protesters protecting
a police officer Saturday night who had done

00:07:06.219 --> 00:07:09.199
nothing wrong and was separated from his squad.

00:07:09.199 --> 00:07:13.330
This is why it was great to see video from,
I believe it was Brooklyn last night with

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:17.229
organizers saying do not interact with the
police.

00:07:17.229 --> 00:07:18.699
We are not here for that.

00:07:18.699 --> 00:07:21.759
We are here to get our message out.

00:07:21.759 --> 00:07:23.389
Do not incite violence.

00:07:23.389 --> 00:07:26.270
If you see anyone inciting violence, call
them out.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:30.209
And, you know, in the midst of this, there's
this really ugly thing that's going on where

00:07:30.209 --> 00:07:36.430
there are people sort of making the focus
that the protesters are from out of town.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:39.759
And at one point, there was some wrong report
that more than half of protesters in some

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:41.249
place were from out of town.

00:07:41.249 --> 00:07:44.809
Another report, it said, seems like one out
of seven were from out of town.

00:07:44.809 --> 00:07:46.860
Now, sometimes that could mean the next town
over.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.110
It's not really a big deal.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:54.179
But the point is this out of town narrative
is being used to imply that you merely have

00:07:54.179 --> 00:07:59.270
people from other communities who are not
coming in to protest because they actually

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:04.289
are concerned about police brutality and relations
or whatever, but that they are coming into

00:08:04.289 --> 00:08:06.759
provoke or to loot or to steal.

00:08:06.759 --> 00:08:11.139
You could be concerned about what went on
and drive from another state.

00:08:11.139 --> 00:08:13.319
And it also appears to be completely untrue.

00:08:13.319 --> 00:08:18.800
So we've got to be really understanding of
how some of these narratives are being used

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:22.729
to distort and discredit real justifiable
anger.

00:08:22.729 --> 00:08:27.490
We also have to recognize the growing number
of protests that are saying we are not here

00:08:27.490 --> 00:08:29.550
to do violence at all.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:32.430
And we are not here to do violence to the
police.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:40.750
But we have to realize that the anger is justified
given the systemic dynamics that exist.

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:41.753
That's sort of my sense of it right now.

00:08:41.753 --> 00:08:41.779
I'm glad to hear from you about it as well

